Let $E$ have independent columns and let $R$ satisfy that $E^tR$ is symmetric. With $E^\dagger=(E^tE)^{-1}E^t$, the matrix $$\label{sc:correction-formula}
 H=R E^\dagger+(E^\dagger)^tR^t
       -(E^\dagger)^t(E^tR)E^\dagger$$ is symmetric and satisfies $HE=R$. Its norm is bounded by a locally uniform constant times $\left\lVert R\right\rVert$ when $E$ ranges over a bounded set with its smallest singular value bounded below.

Suppose $u,v$ are smooth with independent gradients on a connected open region. Let $r^1,r^2$ be smooth densities supported in a fixed compact subset of that region and satisfying $$\label{bl:three-moments}
 \int r^1=\int r^2=
 \int(vr^1-ur^2)=0.$$ Then there is a compactly supported smooth symmetric matrix density $H$ such that $$\label{bl:correction-equations}
 \mathop{\mathrm{div}}(H\nabla u)=r^1,\qquad \mathop{\mathrm{div}}(H\nabla v)=r^2.$$ For a fixed finite system of regular coordinate charts and supports, the construction has bounds in smooth norms. These bounds persist under sufficiently small smooth perturbations of the charts and fields.

